{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "nist-gcr-17-917-46v3:p64:c0:q4:abstain:1", "format": true, "correct": false, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 123, "length": -0.0, "total": 0.1, "text": "Axial growth and lengthening effects due to flexure and curvature can be captured using concrete compressive creep and shrinkage models, and by incorporating effective flexural rigidity equations such as those provided by Kwon [nist-gcr-17-917-46v3:p47:c1]. These equations, which account for peak prior lateral drift ratio, axial load ratio, and longitudinal reinforcement ratio, extend the secant stiffness model to pre-yield levels and include other key parameters [nist-gcr-17-917-46v3:p47:c1]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 127, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "For a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners, the effective width (we) is determined using the formula we = 2c1bsp + c3wsp + cs, where c1 = 0.18, c2 = 1.31, c3 = 0.0, and cs is the width of the stiffeners [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]. Since the web has two longitudinal stiffeners, c2 is not used [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 118, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "The effective width (we) for a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners under compression is determined as follows: calculate w per Article E6.1.3, calculate we based on two longitudinal stiffeners, and since the web has two longitudinal stiffeners, use c1 = 0.18, c2 = 1.31, and c3 = 0.0 in Eq. (6.9.4.2.2b-4) [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 71, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "The effective width (we) for a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners under compression in a steel box-section member is calculated as the sum of the widths of the two longitudinal stiffeners plus the perimeter of the stiffened plate divided by 4 [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 94, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "The effective width (we) for a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners is calculated per Article 6.9.4.2.2b, where c1 = 0.18, c2 = 1.31, and c3 = 0.0, applied to a web panel with two longitudinal stiffeners between diaphragms [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 131, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "The effective width (we) for a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners under compression is calculated per Article 6.9.4.2.2b [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0][fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]. For two longitudinal stiffeners, c1 = 0.18, c2 = 1.31, and c3 = 0.0, with the web panel considered slender for these calculations [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 54, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "The effective width (we) for a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners is determined as half the space between stiffeners, as there are two longitudinal stiffeners [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 140, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "For a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners under compression, the effective width (we) is determined as the width between the inside faces of flanges or transverse stiffeners, calculated based on the slender panels between them, with constants c1 = 0.18, c2 = 1.31, and c3 = 0.0 for two longitudinal stiffeners under non-uniform stress conditions, using Eq. (6.9.4.2.2b-4) [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0][fhwa-hif19063:p277:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "eval", "task_id": "fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 123, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "For a longitudinally stiffened web plate with two longitudinal stiffeners under compression, the effective width (we) is calculated per Article 6.9.4.2.2b due to the slender nature of the longitudinally stiffened web plate panels. For two longitudinal stiffeners, c1 = 0.18, c2 = 1.31, and c3 = 0.0 are used in Eq. (6.9.4.2.2b-4) to determine we [fhwa-hif19063:p278:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 85, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness data cannot be used for fracture mechanics analysis quantitatively because direct toughness values measured in terms of JIc or KIc at operating temperatures are required, which are not known for these splices [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0][nist-gcr-22-917-51:p134:c1]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 119, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness data cannot be used in a quantitative manner for fracture mechanics analysis because it cannot account for differences in deposition rates, number of weld passes, and heat input, which affect in situ toughness compared to CVN test results. Additionally, toughness values measured in terms of JIc or KIc at operating temperatures are needed for fracture mechanics analysis, not CVN values [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0][nist-gcr-22-917-51:p134:c1]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 183, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness data cannot be directly used in quantitative fracture mechanics analysis because it cannot account for different deposition rates, weld passes, and heat input, which affect in situ toughness [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. It also requires toughness data in terms of JIc or KIc for accurate analysis [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. Indirect estimation via empirical correlations, not verified for the materials in pre-Northridge splices, is necessary [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. Additionally, multipass welds like PJP or CJP welds can introduce heterogeneous, brittle zones [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p134:c1]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 67, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness data cannot in itself be used quantitatively for fracture mechanics analysis because it cannot accurately predict the fracture initiation and propagation parameters like JIc or KIc, which require test data at operating temperatures and structural conditions [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 121, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness data cannot be directly used for fracture mechanics analysis because it cannot be quantitatively applied without first correlating it to fracture toughness measurements like JIc or KIc [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. Moreover, the in situ toughness of welded materials may differ from CVN values due to factors such as deposition rates, weld passes, and heat input, and it is crucial to use empirical correlations for indirect estimation [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 118, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness cannot be used in a quantitative manner in fracture mechanics analysis because toughness values measured in terms of JIc or KIc at operating temperatures are needed [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. Additionally, the CVN toughness of weld material extracted from the pre-Northridge beam-to-column connection has not been independently verified as representative due to differences in deposition rates, weld passes, and heat input [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": true, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 108, "length": -0.0, "total": 1.0, "text": "CVN toughness cannot be used directly for fracture mechanics analysis because it cannot be quantitatively used in such analyses; toughness values measured in terms of JIc or KIc at operational temperatures are needed [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. Moreover, CVN toughness may differ from in situ toughness due to deposition rate, number of weld passes, and heat input [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]."}
{"step": 0, "mode": "train", "task_id": "nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0:q4:grounded:1", "format": true, "correct": false, "terminated": true, "n_tokens": 173, "length": -0.0, "total": 0.1, "text": "CVN toughness alone cannot be used for fracture mechanics analysis because it cannot be quantitatively applied without knowledge of toughness values in terms of JIc or KIc [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]. Correlations like the Master Curve method require JIc or KIc data [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p134:c1][nist-gcr-22-917-51:p134:c0], which are not directly available for pre-Northridge materials. Additionally, CVN toughness may differ from the in situ toughness due to deposition rates, number of weld passes, and heat input [nist-gcr-22-917-51:p136:c0]."}
